# json — JSON, in pure Luce.
#
# Parse a document, walk it, read the leaves, and print it back out.
# Nothing here is a builtin, nothing here imports anything, and
# nothing here touches the host: `parse` takes a string and answers a
# document, and where the string came from is the caller's business.
#
# **Written against the normative documents.**  The grammar is RFC
# 8259 — §2 the structural characters and the four whitespace bytes,
# §3 the three literal names, §4 objects, §5 arrays, §6 numbers, §7
# strings and their escapes, §8.1 encoding, §8.2 unpaired surrogates,
# §9 nesting limits.  ECMA-404 says the same grammar in other words
# and is cited where the two documents leave a reader a choice.  Every
# rule below names the clause it implements, and the specification
# (`src/luce/specs/json_spec.zig`) proves each one on both engines.
#
# **Lazy, in simdjson's On-Demand sense.**  A parse walks the text
# once, checks that every byte of it is grammatical, and records where
# each value's text begins and ends — it does not turn `"1e3"` into a
# double or `"é"` into é until somebody asks.  A document that is
# read for one field pays for one field.  The leaf's **original text**
# is what a node holds, so `node.raw()` is always exactly what the
# author wrote, and `write` puts the same bytes back.
#
# **A document is flat, and a node is a value that points into it.**
# `Document` owns one `list(Node)` holding every value in document
# order; a `Node` is a plain value — a kind, two spans of text, and
# three numbers — so it copies for nothing, returns from any function,
# and needs no ownership verb anywhere.  Each node records `beyond`,
# the index one past the last node of its own subtree, which is
# simdjson's tape: the first child of a container is the node after
# it, and the next sibling of any value is its `beyond`.  Navigation
# is therefore a method on the *document* (`doc.get(node, "name")`),
# because a node on its own does not know where the rest of the
# document lives.
#
# That shape is not a preference, it is what the language allows: a
# nested tree of `list(Node)` children cannot answer `get(key) ->
# Node?` at all, because returning an object-carrying struct read out
# of a container is refused (OWNERSHIP.md S17, S22) and returning a
# `copy` of one would deep-copy the whole subtree on every field
# access.  A flat document has one heap object for a document of any
# size, and the reading path allocates nothing.
#
# **Ownership.**  The binding that receives the document owns it, and
# the end of that scope frees it — one list, however large the
# document.  A `Node` owns nothing at all: it is a value, and copying
# one copies a kind, two short strings and three numbers.  A node
# belongs to the document it came from; handing one to a *different*
# document reads whatever that document holds at the same index, or
# traps on the way, so keep the pair together the way you would a
# slice and the thing it slices.
#
# **Reading a file** is three calls, and the module deliberately makes
# none of them:
#
#     let bytes = try files.read_bytes(path)
#     let text = strings.from_bytes(bytes) else ""
#     let doc = try json.parse(text)
#
# **UTF-8 needs no checking here** (RFC 8259 §8.1).  The input is a
# Luce string, which is valid UTF-8 by construction, so the encoding
# question every other parser has to answer was answered before the
# text arrived.  What is left is the grammar, and that is all this
# module checks.
#
# **The calls this module makes that other parsers argue about**, each
# with its reason:
#
# * **Nesting is bounded at 128** (RFC 8259 §9 explicitly allows a
#   parser to set a limit).  The parse itself is iterative and would
#   not care, but a document is walked by callers, and loom lets a
#   Luce program nest 128 calls before it traps `call_depth_exceeded`
#   (`src/apps/host.zig`).  Accepting a document deeper than that
#   would hand a caller a tree no recursive function of theirs can
#   walk.  serde_json's default recursion limit is the same number,
#   arrived at from the other direction.
# * **A duplicate member name resolves to the last one written**
#   (RFC 8259 §4: names SHOULD be unique, and the behaviour when they
#   are not is unpredictable).  Every mainstream parser — JavaScript's
#   `JSON.parse`, Python's `json`, Go's `encoding/json` — keeps the
#   last, and so does `get`.  The *document* is not edited to match:
#   `count`, `items` and `keys` still show every member as written,
#   because a reader that wants to know a document repeats itself
#   should be able to find out.
# * **An unpaired surrogate escape is refused** (RFC 8259 §8.2 warns
#   about them; ECMA-404 permits the code unit sequence).  A Luce
#   string is UTF-8 and there is no UTF-8 for half a surrogate pair,
#   so the honest answers are "refuse" or "quietly substitute U+FFFD",
#   and this module refuses by name at the byte where it happened.
#   A well-formed pair — `𝄞` — is one codepoint, 𝄞, and is
#   read as one.
# * **`as_long` reads the notation, not the value.**  `42` answers
#   42; `4.2`, `42.0` and `4.2e1` all answer `none`, and are read with
#   `as_double`.  A number written as a real is a real, and answering
#   absence says so — where truncating would throw information away
#   silently and rounding would invent it.  An integer too large for
#   `long` also answers `none`, for the same reason `parse_int` does.
# * **NaN and Infinity are not JSON** (RFC 8259 §6 lists exactly one
#   number grammar and no names in it), so they are refused like any
#   other unknown word.
#
# Errors name a byte offset, because that is the position the parser
# genuinely has and a line and column would be a second thing to keep
# right.  `parse` answers `Document!` and not `Document?` because
# there are many distinct ways for a document to be wrong and the one
# it met is worth carrying (docs/FAILURE.md).

# --- the vocabulary of the grammar -----------------------------------
#
# RFC 8259 §2: the six structural characters, the two quoting bytes,
# and nothing else in this module is written as a bare number.

private let quote_byte: byte = 34
private let backslash: byte = 92
private let brace_open: byte = 123
private let brace_close: byte = 125
private let bracket_open: byte = 91
private let bracket_close: byte = 93
private let colon: byte = 58
private let comma: byte = 44
private let minus: byte = 45
private let plus: byte = 43
private let point: byte = 46
private let digit_zero: byte = 48
private let digit_nine: byte = 57

# RFC 8259 §9 leaves the limit to the parser; this one is loom's own
# call-depth policy, so every document this module accepts is a
# document a recursive Luce function can walk.
private let max_depth = 128

# --- what a value is -------------------------------------------------

# The six kinds of JSON value (RFC 8259 §3 the literal names, §4
# objects, §5 arrays, §6 numbers, §7 strings).
#
# Five carry the spec's own word.  The sixth cannot: `string` is a
# reserved name in Luce (it is the type, and the conversion
# constructor named for it), so JSON's string kind is spelled `text`
# here — the same word `strings.from_bytes` uses for the same thing.
enum Kind:
    object
    array
    text
    number
    boolean
    null

# One value in a document: what kind it is, the text it was written
# as, and where it sits.
#
# A plain value with no object fields, so it copies for nothing and
# crosses every boundary without a verb.  The fields are the module's
# own — a program reads a node through the methods below and gets one
# only by parsing a document (docs/VISIBILITY.md §6).
struct Node:
    # What the value is.
    private tag: Kind
    # A leaf's text exactly as it was written, quotes and escapes and
    # all; "" for an object or an array, whose text is their children.
    private source: string
    # This value's member name as it was written, quotes included, when
    # it is a member of an object; "" when it is not one.  An object
    # member whose name is empty holds the two bytes of `""`, so the
    # two cases never collide.
    private label: string
    # Where this value sits in its document.
    private position: long
    # One past the last node of this value's subtree — the index of the
    # next sibling, and simdjson's tape link.
    private beyond: long
    # How many members or elements; 0 for every leaf.
    private size: long

    # Which of the six kinds this is.  Worth a `match`: without an
    # `else` the compiler checks all six are handled.
    func kind(self) -> Kind:
        return self.tag

    # The value's text exactly as it was written — the escapes still
    # escaped, the number still in its own notation.  A container
    # answers "": its text is its children, and `doc.write(node)` is
    # what puts that back together.
    func raw(self) -> string:
        return self.source

    # How many members an object has, or elements an array has.  A
    # leaf answers 0.  Members are counted as written, so a document
    # that names one twice counts it twice.
    func count(self) -> long:
        return self.size

    # This value's member name, decoded, when it is a member of an
    # object; "" when it is not one.  The decoding happens here rather
    # than at parse time, like every other unpacking in this module.
    func key(self) -> string:
        if len(self.label) == 0:
            return ""
        return unescape(self.label)

    # True for JSON's `null` and nothing else — which is a different
    # question from "is this value missing", and that is the point of
    # having both (`doc.get` answers absence).
    func is_null(self) -> bool:
        return self.tag == Kind.null

    # `true` or `false`; absent for every other kind.
    func as_bool(self) -> bool?:
        if self.tag != Kind.boolean:
            return none
        return self.source == "true"

    # The number as a whole number — absent when the value is not a
    # number, when it was written with a fraction or an exponent, or
    # when it does not fit a `long`.
    func as_long(self) -> long?:
        if self.tag != Kind.number:
            return none
        if not written_whole(self.source):
            return none
        return parse_int(self.source)

    # The number as a double — absent when the value is not a number,
    # and when the magnitude is past what a double can hold.
    func as_double(self) -> double?:
        if self.tag != Kind.number:
            return none
        return parse_float(self.source)

    # The string with its escapes decoded (RFC 8259 §7); absent for
    # every other kind.  This is where a `\n` becomes a newline and a
    # `𝄞` becomes 𝄞.
    func as_text(self) -> string?:
        if self.tag != Kind.text:
            return none
        return unescape(self.source)

# A parsed document: every value in it, in the order they were
# written, and the root at the front.
#
# The one heap object this module makes.  It owns nothing else, and
# the binding that receives it frees it.
struct Document:
    private nodes: list(Node)

    # The one top-level value (RFC 8259 §2: a document is one value).
    func root(self) -> Node:
        return self.nodes[0]

    # The member of an object named `name`, or absent — for a name
    # that is not there and for a value that is not an object, which
    # are the same news to a caller who was looking for a field.
    #
    # The members are scanned in order, the way simdjson's On-Demand
    # interface scans them: an object is a short list in practice and
    # an index over it would cost every document to serve some.  The
    # scan runs to the end rather than stopping at the first hit, so a
    # document that names a member twice resolves to the last one, as
    # every mainstream parser does.
    #
    # `has` would be the other half of this pair, and it is a reserved
    # name (it is `m.has(k)`, the map method); `doc.get(node, name) !=
    # none` is the question, and it is the same one call.
    func get(self, node: Node, name: string) -> Node?:
        if node.tag != Kind.object:
            return none
        var found: long = -1
        var at = node.position + 1
        for i in range(0, node.size):
            let child = self.nodes[at]
            if label_is(child.label, name):
                found = at
            at = child.beyond
        if found < 0:
            return none
        return self.nodes[found]

    # The element at `index` of an array, or the member at that
    # position of an object.  Out of range traps: `node.count()` is
    # right there, and asking past it is a bug rather than news
    # (docs/FAILURE.md).
    #
    # Reaching element `i` walks `i` siblings, so a loop that wants
    # every element should ask `items` for them once instead.
    func at(self, node: Node, index: long) -> Node:
        if index < 0 or index >= node.size:
            trap(f"json: no element {index} in a value of {node.size}")
        var found = node.position + 1
        for i in range(0, index):
            found = self.nodes[found].beyond
        return self.nodes[found]

    # Every member or element, in the order written; a fresh list the
    # caller owns.  A leaf answers an empty one.
    func items(self, node: Node) -> list(Node):
        var out: list(Node) = []
        var at = node.position + 1
        for i in range(0, node.size):
            let child = self.nodes[at]
            out.append(child)
            at = child.beyond
        return out

    # Every member name of an object, decoded, in the order written —
    # duplicates included, because they are in the document.  Anything
    # that is not an object answers an empty list.
    func keys(self, node: Node) -> list(string):
        var out: list(string) = []
        if node.tag != Kind.object:
            return out
        var at = node.position + 1
        for i in range(0, node.size):
            let child = self.nodes[at]
            out.append(unescape(child.label))
            at = child.beyond
        return out

    # The value as JSON text, with nothing between the tokens.
    #
    # Not a byte-for-byte echo of the input — the whitespace a document
    # arrived with is not kept — but every *token* is the one that was
    # read, escapes and number notation and all, so parsing the answer
    # gives the same document and writing that gives the same bytes.
    func write(self, node: Node) -> string:
        return render(self, node, 0)

    # The same, indented: every member and element on its own line,
    # `spaces` further in than its container.  `spaces` of 0 is
    # `write`.
    func pretty(self, node: Node, spaces: long) -> string:
        if spaces < 0:
            trap("json: pretty needs spaces >= 0")
        return render(self, node, spaces)

# --- parsing ---------------------------------------------------------

# Read one JSON document (RFC 8259 §2: exactly one value, with
# whitespace allowed around it).
#
# Everything that can be wrong with the text is an error naming the
# byte it happened at.  Nothing is decoded here beyond what checking
# the grammar requires: the values keep their text and unpack when
# they are read.
func parse(content: string) -> Document!:
    var nodes: list(Node) = []
    var at = skip_blank(content, 0)
    if at >= len(content):
        error("json: there is no value in the text")
    at = try scan_value(nodes, content, at)
    at = skip_blank(content, at)
    if at != len(content):
        error(f"json: text after the document's one value, at byte {at}")
    return Document(nodes = give nodes)

# The whole grammar, as one loop over the text with an explicit stack
# of open containers.
#
# Iterative rather than recursive on purpose: recursion would make the
# depth a program may parse a function of the host's call-depth policy
# and the shape of this file, which is not something a document should
# depend on.  The limit is a number this module states (`max_depth`)
# and reports by name.
#
# `open` holds the node index of every container still waiting for its
# closing byte; `tally` counts what has gone into each of them.
#
# The growing document is passed as the list itself rather than as the
# `Document` that will own it: closing a container is `nodes[i].size =
# n`, and a struct read out of a `let`-bound name is not a place to
# assign into — the store would land in the name, which is exactly
# what `let` forbids.  Through the list it lands in the object, which
# is what it means to.  `parse` gives the finished list to the
# document it hands back.
private func scan_value(nodes: list(Node), content: string, start: long) -> long!:
    var at = start
    var open: list(long) = []
    var tally: list(long) = []
    while true:
        at = skip_blank(content, at)
        # RFC 8259 §4: inside an object every value is preceded by a
        # string and a colon.  Whether that is the case is a question
        # about the innermost open container and nothing else, which
        # is why it is asked here rather than at each of the two
        # places that lead to a value.
        var label = ""
        if len(open) > 0 and nodes[open[len(open) - 1]].tag == Kind.object:
            if at >= len(content) or content.byte_at(at) != quote_byte:
                error(f"json: a member name must be a string, at byte {at}")
            let name_at = at
            at = try scan_string(content, at)
            label = content[name_at:at]
            at = skip_blank(content, at)
            if at >= len(content) or content.byte_at(at) != colon:
                error(f"json: a member name must be followed by a colon, at byte {at}")
            at = skip_blank(content, at + 1)
        if at >= len(content):
            error(f"json: a value was expected at byte {at}")

        let head = content.byte_at(at)
        if head == brace_open or head == bracket_open:
            if len(open) >= max_depth:
                error(f"json: values nested deeper than {max_depth}, at byte {at}")
            var tag = Kind.array
            if head == brace_open:
                tag = Kind.object
            let opened = len(nodes)
            nodes.append(Node(
                tag = tag,
                source = "",
                label = label,
                position = opened,
                beyond = opened + 1,
                size = 0,
            ))
            open.append(opened)
            tally.append(0)
            at = skip_blank(content, at + 1)
            # An empty container closes where it opened; anything else
            # sends the loop round for its first member or element.
            if at < len(content) and content.byte_at(at) == closer_of(tag):
                at += 1
                close_top(nodes, open, tally)
            else:
                continue
        else:
            let value_at = at
            var tag = Kind.null
            if head == quote_byte:
                tag = Kind.text
                at = try scan_string(content, at)
            elif head == minus or is_digit(head):
                tag = Kind.number
                at = try scan_number(content, at)
            elif word_at(content, at, "true"):
                tag = Kind.boolean
                at += 4
            elif word_at(content, at, "false"):
                tag = Kind.boolean
                at += 5
            elif word_at(content, at, "null"):
                tag = Kind.null
                at += 4
            else:
                error(f"json: a value was expected at byte {at}")
            let leaf = len(nodes)
            nodes.append(Node(
                tag = tag,
                source = content[value_at:at],
                label = label,
                position = leaf,
                beyond = leaf + 1,
                size = 0,
            ))

        # A value has just been finished.  Count it in whatever
        # contains it, and close every container it was the last of —
        # each of which is a finished value in its own right, which is
        # why this is a loop and not a step.
        while true:
            if len(open) == 0:
                return at
            tally[len(tally) - 1] += 1
            at = skip_blank(content, at)
            let top = nodes[open[len(open) - 1]]
            if at >= len(content):
                error(f"json: the text ends inside the {string(top.tag)} opened at byte {top.position}")
            let mark = content.byte_at(at)
            if mark == comma:
                at += 1
                break
            elif mark == closer_of(top.tag):
                at += 1
                close_top(nodes, open, tally)
            else:
                error(f"json: a comma or a close was expected at byte {at}")
    # The loop above leaves only by returning or by reporting, and the
    # compiler cannot see that a `while true:` never falls out — so the
    # promise is written here, where it can be read.
    trap("json: the parse loop fell out of a loop it cannot leave")

# Finish the innermost open container: it holds what the tally counted
# and its subtree is everything appended since it opened.
private func close_top(nodes: list(Node), open: list(long), tally: list(long)):
    let last = len(open) - 1
    let closed = open[last]
    nodes[closed].size = tally[last]
    nodes[closed].beyond = len(nodes)
    open.remove(last)
    tally.remove(last)

# RFC 8259 §2: whitespace is exactly these four bytes — space,
# horizontal tab, line feed and carriage return.  Nothing else is
# whitespace, which is why a vertical tab or a form feed between two
# tokens is an error rather than a shrug.
private func skip_blank(content: string, from: long) -> long:
    var at = from
    while at < len(content):
        let c = content.byte_at(at)
        if c == 32 or c == 9 or c == 10 or c == 13:
            at += 1
        else:
            return at
    return at

# RFC 8259 §7.  Answers the byte after the closing quote, having
# checked every escape on the way — so `as_text` can decode without
# asking any question twice.
private func scan_string(content: string, from: long) -> long!:
    var at = from + 1
    while at < len(content):
        let c = content.byte_at(at)
        if c == quote_byte:
            return at + 1
        elif c == backslash:
            at = try scan_escape(content, at)
        elif c < 32:
            # §7: the control characters must be escaped.  A raw
            # newline inside a string is the usual way to meet this.
            error(f"json: an unescaped control character inside a string, at byte {at}")
        else:
            at += 1
    error(f"json: the string opened at byte {from} is never closed")

# RFC 8259 §7's eight escapes.  Answers the byte after the escape.
private func scan_escape(content: string, from: long) -> long!:
    if from + 1 >= len(content):
        error(f"json: the text ends inside an escape, at byte {from}")
    let c = content.byte_at(from + 1)
    if c == 117:
        # \uXXXX.  A high surrogate is half of a codepoint and must be
        # followed by its low half (§7); a low one on its own is not a
        # codepoint at all (§8.2).  Refusing both is what keeps every
        # string this module accepts decodable into UTF-8, which is
        # the only thing a Luce string can be.
        let value = try read_hex4(content, from + 2)
        if value >= 55296 and value <= 56319:
            if from + 7 >= len(content) or content.byte_at(from + 6) != backslash or content.byte_at(from + 7) != 117:
                error(f"json: a high surrogate escape at byte {from} is not followed by its low half")
            let low = try read_hex4(content, from + 8)
            if low < 56320 or low > 57343:
                error(f"json: a high surrogate escape at byte {from} is followed by something that is not a low surrogate")
            return from + 12
        elif value >= 56320 and value <= 57343:
            error(f"json: a low surrogate escape stands alone at byte {from}")
        return from + 6
    elif c == quote_byte or c == backslash or c == 47 or c == 98 or c == 102 or c == 110 or c == 114 or c == 116:
        # " \ / b f n r t — the whole of the rest of §7.
        return from + 2
    error(f"json: unknown escape at byte {from}")

# Four hexadecimal digits, as the number they spell.
private func read_hex4(content: string, from: long) -> long!:
    if from + 4 > len(content):
        error(f"json: an escape at byte {from} wants four hexadecimal digits and the text ends first")
    var value: long = 0
    for i in range(0, 4):
        let c = content.byte_at(from + i)
        var part = -1
        if c >= digit_zero and c <= digit_nine:
            part = c - digit_zero
        elif c >= 97 and c <= 102:
            part = c - 97 + 10
        elif c >= 65 and c <= 70:
            part = c - 65 + 10
        else:
            error(f"json: an escape at byte {from} wants four hexadecimal digits")
        value = value * 16 + part
    return value

# RFC 8259 §6, and the whole of it: an optional minus, an integer part
# that is either a single zero or a digit string not starting with
# one, an optional fraction with at least one digit, and an optional
# exponent with at least one digit.  `.5`, `5.`, `01`, `+1`, `1e`,
# `0x10`, `NaN` and `Infinity` are all outside it, and none of them
# reaches a caller as a number.
private func scan_number(content: string, from: long) -> long!:
    var at = from
    if content.byte_at(at) == minus:
        at += 1
    if at >= len(content) or not is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
        error(f"json: a number needs a digit, at byte {at}")
    if content.byte_at(at) == digit_zero:
        at += 1
        if at < len(content) and is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            error(f"json: a number may not have a leading zero, at byte {from}")
    else:
        while at < len(content) and is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            at += 1
    if at < len(content) and content.byte_at(at) == point:
        at += 1
        if at >= len(content) or not is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            error(f"json: a fraction needs a digit after the point, at byte {at}")
        while at < len(content) and is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            at += 1
    if at < len(content) and (content.byte_at(at) == 101 or content.byte_at(at) == 69):
        at += 1
        if at < len(content) and (content.byte_at(at) == plus or content.byte_at(at) == minus):
            at += 1
        if at >= len(content) or not is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            error(f"json: an exponent needs a digit, at byte {at}")
        while at < len(content) and is_digit(content.byte_at(at)):
            at += 1
    return at

private func is_digit(c: byte) -> bool:
    return c >= digit_zero and c <= digit_nine

# True when `word` stands at `at`.  Byte by byte rather than by slice,
# because a slice that ended inside a character would trap and the
# text after a literal name is whatever the document says it is.
private func word_at(content: string, at: long, word: string) -> bool:
    if at + len(word) > len(content):
        return false
    for i in range(0, len(word)):
        if content.byte_at(at + i) != word.byte_at(i):
            return false
    return true

# Which byte closes a container of this kind.
private func closer_of(tag: Kind) -> long:
    if tag == Kind.object:
        return brace_close
    return bracket_close

# True when a number was written without a fraction and without an
# exponent — the question `as_long` asks.
private func written_whole(source: string) -> bool:
    return source.find_byte(point, 0) < 0 and source.find_byte(101, 0) < 0 and source.find_byte(69, 0) < 0

# --- text, both directions -------------------------------------------

# A quoted, escaped member name compared against a plain one, without
# building anything.  Names hold no escapes in almost every document
# there is, so that case is a length check and a byte compare; the
# rest decode and compare.
private func label_is(label: string, name: string) -> bool:
    if len(label) < 2:
        return false
    if label.find_byte(backslash, 1) < 0:
        if len(label) - 2 != len(name):
            return false
        for i in range(0, len(name)):
            if label.byte_at(1 + i) != name.byte_at(i):
                return false
        return true
    return unescape(label) == name

# A JSON string token — quotes included — as the text it spells (RFC
# 8259 §7).
#
# Total, because the parser already refused every escape this cannot
# decode; the trap at the bottom is that promise written down.  A
# token with no backslash in it is its own contents, and is sliced
# rather than rebuilt.
private func unescape(token: string) -> string:
    let last = len(token) - 1
    if token.find_byte(backslash, 1) < 0:
        return token[1:last]
    var out = new builder()
    var at = 1
    while at < last:
        let c = token.byte_at(at)
        if c != backslash:
            # A run of ordinary bytes goes in whole: it can hold whole
            # characters of any width, and it ends at a backslash or
            # at the closing quote, both of which are ASCII.
            let run = at
            while at < last and token.byte_at(at) != backslash:
                at += 1
            out.append(token[run:at])
        else:
            let e = token.byte_at(at + 1)
            if e == quote_byte or e == backslash or e == 47:
                out.append_ascii(e)
                at += 2
            elif e == 98:
                out.append_ascii(8)
                at += 2
            elif e == 102:
                out.append_ascii(12)
                at += 2
            elif e == 110:
                out.append_ascii(10)
                at += 2
            elif e == 114:
                out.append_ascii(13)
                at += 2
            elif e == 116:
                out.append_ascii(9)
                at += 2
            elif e == 117:
                var code = hex4(token, at + 2)
                at += 6
                if code >= 55296 and code <= 56319:
                    # RFC 8259 §7's example is this exact pair: 𝄞 is
                    # one codepoint written as two escapes.
                    let low = hex4(token, at + 2)
                    code = 65536 + (code - 55296) * 1024 + (low - 56320)
                    at += 6
                out.append(chr(code))
            else:
                trap("json: an escape the parser accepted has no decoding")
    return out.build()

# Four hexadecimal digits the parser has already checked.
private func hex4(token: string, from: long) -> long:
    var value: long = 0
    for i in range(0, 4):
        let c = token.byte_at(from + i)
        if c >= digit_zero and c <= digit_nine:
            value = value * 16 + (c - digit_zero)
        elif c >= 97 and c <= 102:
            value = value * 16 + (c - 97 + 10)
        else:
            value = value * 16 + (c - 65 + 10)
    return value

# Text as a JSON string, quotes and escapes and all (RFC 8259 §7).
#
# The two escapes that are required — the quote and the backslash —
# and the control characters, which the six short names cover where
# they can and `\u00xx` covers where they cannot.  Everything else
# goes in as itself: the solidus needs no escape (§7 permits one and
# does not ask for it), and a character outside ASCII is UTF-8 in the
# output exactly as it was in the input, which is what §8.1 recommends
# and what makes a `\u` escape unnecessary here.
#
# This is the door for a program that builds JSON of its own out of a
# builder rather than parsing any, and it is the same escaping `write`
# puts back around a name.
func quote(content: string) -> string:
    var out = new builder()
    out.append_ascii(quote_byte)
    var run = 0
    var at = 0
    while at < len(content):
        let c = content.byte_at(at)
        if c == quote_byte or c == backslash or c < 32:
            if at > run:
                out.append(content[run:at])
            put_escape(out, c)
            run = at + 1
        at += 1
    out.append(content[run:])
    out.append_ascii(quote_byte)
    return out.build()

private func put_escape(out: builder, c: byte):
    out.append_ascii(backslash)
    if c == quote_byte or c == backslash:
        out.append_ascii(c)
    elif c == 8:
        out.append_ascii(98)
    elif c == 12:
        out.append_ascii(102)
    elif c == 10:
        out.append_ascii(110)
    elif c == 13:
        out.append_ascii(114)
    elif c == 9:
        out.append_ascii(116)
    else:
        # Every other control character, four hexadecimal digits of
        # it.  Lowercase, as JavaScript and Python both write them.
        out.append_ascii(117)
        out.append_ascii(digit_zero)
        out.append_ascii(digit_zero)
        out.append_ascii(hex_digit(c >> 4 & 15))
        out.append_ascii(hex_digit(c & 15))

private func hex_digit(value: int) -> int:
    if value < 10:
        return digit_zero + value
    return 97 + value - 10

# --- writing ---------------------------------------------------------

# One value and its subtree as JSON text, in document order.
#
# The same walk `parse` made, run backwards through the tape: a
# container's first child is the node after it, and every value's next
# sibling is its `beyond`.  Iterative for the same reason the parser
# is — a document 128 deep is one this module accepted, so it has to
# be one this module can print.
private func render(doc: Document, node: Node, spaces: long) -> string:
    var out = new builder()
    var open: list(long) = []
    var left: list(long) = []
    var at = node.position
    while true:
        let current = doc.nodes[at]
        if len(open) > 0:
            let parent = doc.nodes[open[len(open) - 1]]
            if left[len(left) - 1] < parent.size:
                out.append(",")
            put_break(out, spaces, len(open))
            if parent.tag == Kind.object:
                # The name goes back exactly as it arrived, escapes
                # and all: it was checked, so it needs no rewriting.
                out.append(current.label)
                out.append(":")
                if spaces > 0:
                    out.append(" ")
            left[len(left) - 1] -= 1
        if current.tag == Kind.object or current.tag == Kind.array:
            if current.size == 0:
                out.append(empty_of(current.tag))
            else:
                out.append(opener_of(current.tag))
                open.append(at)
                left.append(current.size)
                at += 1
                continue
        else:
            out.append(current.source)
        # The value is written; close every container it completed,
        # innermost first, and go on to whatever follows the outermost
        # of them.
        var last = at
        while len(open) > 0 and left[len(left) - 1] == 0:
            last = open[len(open) - 1]
            open.remove(len(open) - 1)
            left.remove(len(left) - 1)
            put_break(out, spaces, len(open))
            out.append(closer_text(doc.nodes[last].tag))
        if len(open) == 0:
            return out.build()
        at = doc.nodes[last].beyond
    # As in `scan_value`: the loop leaves by returning, and this says so.
    trap("json: the writer fell out of a loop it cannot leave")

# A line break and one level of indentation, or nothing at all when
# the caller asked for no spaces.
private func put_break(out: builder, spaces: long, depth: long):
    if spaces <= 0:
        return
    out.append_ascii(10)
    for i in range(0, spaces * depth):
        out.append_ascii(32)

private func opener_of(tag: Kind) -> string:
    if tag == Kind.object:
        return "{"
    return "["

private func closer_text(tag: Kind) -> string:
    if tag == Kind.object:
        return "}"
    return "]"

private func empty_of(tag: Kind) -> string:
    if tag == Kind.object:
        return "{}"
    return "[]"
